MCQ = [
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.12","title":"Leave Management","page":215,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 says commanders may delegate leave approval normally no lower than whom?","correct":"The first-line supervisor","w1":"Any Airman in the shop","w2":"The installation commander only","w3":"Finance, with no supervisor in the chain","explain":"Paragraph 11.12 says commanders keep final approval and may delegate it, normally no lower than the first-line supervisor. LeaveWeb still routes the request for validation."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.12","title":"Leave Management","page":215,"diff":2,"question":"If LeaveWeb is down, AFH 1 says units may fall back to which form?","correct":"DAF Form 988, Leave Request/Authorization","w1":"DAF Form 847","w2":"AF Form 910 as a leave form","w3":"A plain text message with no record","explain":"Paragraph 11.12 names DAF Form 988 as the fallback when LeaveWeb is unavailable. The 847 is a publication-change form."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.13","title":"Beginning and Ending Leave","page":215,"diff":3,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1 says leave must begin and end where?","correct":"In the local area, the place from which the member commutes daily","w1":"At any airport in the world","w2":"Only on a military installation","w3":"In the member's hometown even if they no longer live there","explain":"Paragraph 11.13 defines the local area as the residence from which the member commutes to the duty station. Leave starts and ends there, including leave en route to a PCS."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.13","title":"Chargeable Leave","page":215,"diff":3,"question":"If a leave period includes a weekend, what does AFH 1 forbid as a way to skip those days?","correct":"Ending leave on Friday and starting it again on Monday","w1":"Charging Saturday and Sunday when they fall inside the leave period","w2":"Asking for an extension before the leave expires","w3":"Returning to the local area to end leave","explain":"Paragraph 11.13 says nonduty days inside leave are chargeable, and a member cannot end leave Friday and restart Monday to dodge the weekend."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.14","title":"Accruing Leave","page":215,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"How much leave does AFH 1 say members accrue for each month of RegAF status?","correct":"2.5 days","w1":"1 day","w2":"5 days","w3":"30 days","explain":"Paragraph 11.14 says members accrue 2.5 days of leave per month of RegAF status. Thirty days is the rough yearly total, not the monthly rate."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.14","title":"Accruing Leave","page":215,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 says the ordinary maximum leave that may be carried into a new fiscal year is how many days?","correct":"60 days, unless a higher balance is specifically authorized","w1":"30 days","w2":"90 days with no exception process","w3":"Unlimited","explain":"Paragraph 11.14 sets a 60-day ordinary cap at fiscal year end. Higher carryover exists only when specifically authorized."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.14","title":"Accruing Leave","page":215,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says members do not earn leave in which status?","correct":"Absence without leave, confinement from a court-martial, excess leave, or appellate leave","w1":"Ordinary annual leave","w2":"Convalescent leave","w3":"A normal weekend at home station","explain":"Paragraph 11.14 lists AWOL, unauthorized status, court-martial confinement, excess leave, and appellate leave as non-accruing. Ordinary leave is the benefit being accrued the rest of the time."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11A","para":"11.3","title":"Military and Family Readiness","page":202,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 places the Military and Family Readiness Center in which kind of role?","correct":"A base agency for member and family readiness services","w1":"The office that writes promotion tests","w2":"A combatant command headquarters","w3":"The court-martial convening authority","explain":"Section 11A describes support agencies such as the Military and Family Readiness Center. Promotion tests and courts sit in other chapters."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11A","para":"11.5","title":"Family Care Plans","page":202,"diff":3,"question":"A family care plan in AFH 1 exists so that what can happen?","correct":"Dependents are cared for if the member must perform military duties","w1":"The member can decline every deployment","w2":"Leave accrual doubles","w3":"The member is excused from standards","explain":"Section 11A covers family care plans as the arrangement that keeps dependents cared for when duty calls. They do not cancel deployability or standards."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11A","para":"11.6","title":"Veterans Affairs Benefits","page":202,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 discusses Veterans Affairs benefits so Airmen know they are what?","correct":"A separate benefits system members should learn before separation, not a substitute for active-duty pay rules","w1":"The same thing as basic pay","w2":"A promotion point category","w3":"Run by the squadron booster club","explain":"Section 11A points members toward VA benefits and health care as transition and veteran benefits. They are not WAPS points or unit fundraisers."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.16","title":"Permissive TDY","page":215,"diff":3,"question":"Permissive temporary duty in the leave chapter is best understood as what?","correct":"An authorized absence for an official reason that is not chargeable leave in the ordinary sense","w1":"The same thing as ordinary annual leave","w2":"A permanent change of station","w3":"A punishment","explain":"Section 11B distinguishes permissive TDY from chargeable leave. It is not a PCS order and it is not nonjudicial punishment."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.17","title":"Passes","page":215,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 treats a regular pass as different from leave because a pass is what?","correct":"A short authorized absence that is not the same accounting bucket as chargeable leave","w1":"Always a 30-day vacation","w2":"A foreign travel entitlement with per diem","w3":"An automatic promotion","explain":"Section 11B separates regular and special passes from leave accrual and chargeable leave. A pass is not a PCS entitlement or a stripe."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11C","para":"11.18","title":"Military Associations","page":219,"diff":2,"question":"Private military associations appear in AFH 1 so Airmen remember what limit?","correct":"Official duty and government systems are not the association's property","w1":"Membership is mandatory for promotion","w2":"Associations outrank the commander","w3":"Dues are collected by finance as basic pay","explain":"Section 11C discusses associations as private organizations. They do not replace the chain of command or become a promotion requirement."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11A","para":"11.10","title":"American Red Cross","page":202,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 includes the American Red Cross in the benefits chapter mainly for what function?","correct":"Emergency communication and verification when families need to reach a member","w1":"Writing enlisted promotion tests","w2":"Approving courts-martial","w3":"Setting basic pay charts","explain":"The benefits section lists the Red Cross as an emergency-communication resource. Pay charts, tests, and trials belong to other systems."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11B","para":"11.13","title":"Leave Recall","page":215,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says a unit commander may recall a member from leave for what reason?","correct":"Military necessity or the best interest of the Air Force","w1":"Any coworker's preference","w2":"To save the member's leave balance without a duty reason","w3":"Because the weekend looks busy","explain":"Paragraph 11.13 allows recall for military necessity or the best interest of the Air Force. If leave resumes afterward, new authorization is required."},
{"ch":11,"sec":"11D","para":"11.21","title":"Filling Civilian Jobs","page":220,"diff":3,"question":"Civilian hiring discussion in AFH 1 Chapter 11 is there so uniformed members understand what?","correct":"Civilian employment has its own hiring, pay, and labor rules","w1":"Civilians use the enlisted WAPS board","w2":"Civilians accrue 2.5 days of military leave","w3":"Civilians are promoted under AFI 36-2502","explain":"Section 11D summarizes civilian programs, including filling jobs and labor relations. Those rules are not the enlisted promotion system."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12A","para":"12.1","title":"Financial Responsibilities","page":225,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 quotes AFI 1-1 to compare fiscal fitness with what?","correct":"Physical fitness, as part of overall well-being","w1":"Drill and ceremony scores","w2":"Aircraft tail numbers","w3":"Ribbon precedence","explain":"Paragraph 12.1 says fiscal fitness matters to well-being in the same spirit as physical fitness. The chapter then covers budgets, debt, the LES, and the travel card."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12A","para":"12.3","title":"Leave and Earnings Statement","page":225,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1 identifies the military Leave and Earnings Statement as which form?","correct":"DFAS Form 702","w1":"DAF Form 988","w2":"AF Form 910","w3":"DAF Form 847","explain":"Paragraph 12.3 identifies the LES as DFAS Form 702. DAF Form 988 is a leave form, and the 847 changes publications."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12A","para":"12.2","title":"Military Pay","page":225,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says the military pay date is used to determine what?","correct":"Length of service for pay","w1":"The promotion board score","w2":"The fitness category","w3":"Which combatant command owns the member","explain":"Paragraph 12.2 says the pay date sets length of service for pay and can be adjusted for certain prior service or lost time. It is not a WAPS score."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12B","para":"12.19","title":"Government Travel Charge Card","page":231,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"Unless exempted, AFH 1 says DoD travelers must use the travel card for what?","correct":"Official travel expenses such as lodging, transportation, and authorized rental cars","w1":"Grocery shopping at home station","w2":"Personal holiday gifts","w3":"A family member's tuition","explain":"Paragraph 12.19 requires the GTCC for expenses of official government travel unless the traveler is exempted. Personal spending at home is not that use."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12B","para":"12.19","title":"Government Travel Charge Card","page":231,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says ATM cash advances on the travel card should not be taken earlier than when?","correct":"Three working days before scheduled travel","w1":"Thirty days before travel","w2":"The day after returning","w3":"Any payday, travel or not","explain":"Paragraph 12.19 limits ATM advances to no earlier than three working days before travel and to expenses that are exempt from mandatory card use, such as meals and incidentals."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12B","para":"12.19","title":"Government Travel Charge Card","page":231,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says the cardholder pays travel-card charges, reimbursable or not, by what method?","correct":"Split disbursement","w1":"Ignoring the bill until the next PCS","w2":"Having the commander pay it from a personal account","w3":"Trading the balance for leave days","explain":"Paragraph 12.19 says the cardholder pays all charges through split disbursement. The refund feature saves the department money, but the member still owes the charges."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12C","para":"12.22","title":"Unit Manpower Document","page":235,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 describes the Unit Manpower Document as what?","correct":"The statement of funded manpower requirements for the unit","w1":"A leave calendar","w2":"A decoration citation","w3":"A fitness score sheet","explain":"Section 12C presents the UMD as the unit's manpower requirements document. It is not a leave log or an evaluation."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12D","para":"12.28","title":"PPBE","page":238,"diff":3,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1 says the Planning, Programming, Budgeting, and Execution process has which phases?","correct":"Planning, programming, budgeting, and execution","w1":"Identify, assess, control, implement, and supervise","w2":"Forming, storming, norming, and performing","w3":"Unfreezing, changing, and refreezing","explain":"Paragraph 12.28 names four PPBE phases: planning, programming, budgeting, and execution. The other lists are risk management, team stages, and Lewin's change model."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12D","para":"12.28","title":"PPBE","page":238,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says the key product of the programming phase is what?","correct":"A balanced, capabilities-based Program Objective Memorandum","w1":"The Airman's Creed","w2":"An individual's LES","w3":"A single travel voucher","explain":"Paragraph 12.28 says programming builds the POM and matches resources to requirements. The LES and travel vouchers are individual pay documents."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12D","para":"12.27","title":"Fiscal Year","page":238,"diff":1,"question":"AFH 1 says the budget program operates on a fiscal year that runs when?","correct":"1 October through 30 September","w1":"1 January through 31 December","w2":"1 July through 30 June","w3":"15 February through 14 February","explain":"The resource section says the fiscal year runs from 1 October through the following 30 September. That is also why the WAPS catalog date of 1 October matters."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12D","para":"12.31","title":"Fraud, Waste, and Abuse","page":238,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 treats fraud, waste, and abuse prevention as whose business?","correct":"Every Airman who sees resources being misused, not only auditors","w1":"Only the wing historian","w2":"Only members on a deployment","w3":"Only civilian contractors","explain":"Section 12D makes resource stewardship, including fraud, waste, and abuse, a force-wide duty. Hiding a loss is not the program."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12A","para":"12.1","title":"Financial Responsibilities","page":225,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 names two key pieces of individual financial responsibility as what?","correct":"A personal budget and managing debt","w1":"Ignoring the LES until PCS","w2":"Lending the travel card to a friend","w3":"Skipping vouchers to save time","explain":"Paragraph 12.1 highlights a personal budget and debt management, plus reviewing the LES and filing vouchers on time."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12E","para":"12.33","title":"Environmental Responsibilities","page":244,"diff":2,"question":"Environmental commitment shows up in the resources chapter because AFH 1 ties it to what?","correct":"Stewardship of resources while accomplishing the mission","w1":"A replacement for operational security","w2":"A promotion point category","w3":"Uniform tailoring","explain":"Section 12E frames environmental responsibility as part of resource stewardship. It does not replace OPSEC or become a WAPS math factor."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12B","para":"12.16","title":"PCS Allowances","page":231,"diff":3,"question":"PCS allowances in AFH 1 exist to do what?","correct":"Pay authorized costs of an official move, not personal side trips","w1":"Fund any vacation taken in the same year","w2":"Replace basic pay for the month of the move","w3":"Erase excess household goods weight automatically","explain":"Section 12B covers official PCS and TDY entitlements. Personal travel that is not authorized on the order is not the entitlement."},
{"ch":12,"sec":"12C","para":"12.20","title":"Manpower Requirements","page":235,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says manpower requirements are built to reflect what?","correct":"The people needed to do the mission, documented and validated","w1":"Whoever happens to be on station that Friday","w2":"Only officer billets","w3":"A contractor's preferred headcount with no review","explain":"Section 12C explains that manpower requirements and the UMD are validated statements of need, not a casual headcount."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.11","title":"Three-Stage Change","page":252,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","demo":True,"question":"AFH 1 attributes the three-stage change process of unfreezing, changing, and refreezing to whom?","correct":"Kurt Lewin","w1":"John Boyd","w2":"W. Edwards Deming alone, with no stages named","w3":"The Goldwater-Nichols Act","explain":"Paragraph 13.11 credits Kurt Lewin with unfreezing, changing, and refreezing. Goldwater-Nichols is a command-structure law, not a change model."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.11","title":"Unfreezing","page":252,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"In Lewin's model as AFH 1 explains it, unfreezing mainly does what?","correct":"Prepares people by creating an understood need for the change","w1":"Locks the new behavior in place with rewards","w2":"Skips discussion and orders immediate compliance","w3":"Measures only the final metric","explain":"Paragraph 13.11 says unfreezing prepares people and is often neglected. Refreezing is the later step that locks in new norms."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.11","title":"Refreezing","page":252,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1 says refreezing is complete when leaders do what?","correct":"Reinforce the new behavior so it becomes the normal way of working","w1":"Announce the change and leave","w2":"Return to the old process as soon as complaints start","w3":"Hide the results from the work center","explain":"Paragraph 13.11 says refreezing locks in desired outcomes by staying engaged, evaluating results, and positively reinforcing the new behavior."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.10","title":"Resistance to Change","page":252,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"What does AFH 1 say about coercion as a change technique?","correct":"It can force compliance but is not recommended and needs constant oversight","w1":"It is the preferred first step","w2":"It has no long-term cost","w3":"It is the same thing as education and communication","explain":"Paragraph 13.10 says coercion can damage attitudes and is not recommended. Education, participation, and support are the healthier tools in that discussion."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.13","title":"Practical Problem Solving","page":252,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","demo":True,"question":"How many steps does AFH 1 give the practical problem-solving method?","correct":"Eight","w1":"Three","w2":"Five","w3":"Twelve","explain":"Paragraph 13.13 describes an eight-step method from clarifying the problem through standardizing the successful process. Lewin's model is the three-stage cousin, not this count."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.13","title":"Practical Problem Solving","page":252,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1 says which practical problem-solving step is the most commonly neglected?","correct":"Step 8, standardize successful processes","w1":"Step 1, clarify the problem","w2":"Step 4, find the root cause","w3":"Step 5, develop countermeasures","explain":"Paragraph 13.13 says Step 8 is the most commonly neglected, even though it is how improvements get codified. Root-cause error is called the most common mistake, which is a different point."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.13","title":"Practical Problem Solving","page":252,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1 calls the most common mistake in continuous improvement what?","correct":"Picking the wrong root cause","w1":"Writing the problem statement too clearly","w2":"Involving the people who do the work","w3":"Comparing results to the target","explain":"Paragraph 13.13 says incorrect root-cause determination is the most common CI mistake. If targets are missed, the team may need to return to Step 4."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.12","title":"Continuous Improvement","page":252,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1 says Lean focuses on what?","correct":"Workflow, customer value, and removing non-value-added waste","w1":"Reducing variation with a statistical method","w2":"Redesigning the entire business from scratch only","w3":"Finding the single bottleneck and subordinating everything to it","explain":"Paragraph 13.12 assigns waste and flow to Lean, variation to Six Sigma, fundamental redesign to business process reengineering, and bottlenecks to the theory of constraints."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.12","title":"Continuous Improvement","page":252,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"Which methodology does AFH 1 describe as data-driven reduction of process variation?","correct":"Six Sigma","w1":"Lean","w2":"Theory of constraints","w3":"A passing suggestion box with no data","explain":"Paragraph 13.12 says Six Sigma minimizes waste by identifying, controlling, and reducing variation. Lean targets non-value-added activity more directly."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.12","title":"Continuous Improvement","page":252,"diff":4,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1's theory of constraints tells leaders to do what with a bottleneck?","correct":"Identify, exploit, subordinate, elevate, and reassess it","w1":"Ignore it until the fiscal year ends","w2":"Add inspectors only at the end of the line","w3":"Freeze hiring and hope it moves","explain":"Paragraph 13.12 summarizes the theory of constraints as identifying, exploiting, subordinating, elevating, and reassessing constraints."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13A","para":"13.5","title":"Results Focus","page":246,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","question":"Results focus, as AFH 1 uses it in Developing Organizations, means leaders care about what?","correct":"Outcomes tied to the mission, not activity that only looks busy","w1":"The number of meetings held","w2":"How long the slide deck is","w3":"Whether everyone agreed quickly","explain":"Section 13A contrasts real results with motion that does not change the outcome. Busy calendars are not the competency."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13B","para":"13.7","title":"DOTMLPF","page":249,"diff":4,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1 points organizational problem-solvers at DOTMLPF-P, which includes which set?","correct":"Doctrine, organization, training, materiel, leadership and education, personnel, and facilities","w1":"Only money and manpower","w2":"Only the operations plan","w3":"Ranks, ribbons, and reveille","explain":"Paragraph 13.7 uses the DOTMLPF-P lens so leaders do not treat every problem as a people failure or a money failure alone."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13C","para":"13.13","title":"Practical Problem Solving","page":252,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1 says a problem statement should not do what?","correct":"Assume the root cause or the solution in the first sentence","w1":"Use data","w2":"Name where the problem shows up","w3":"State why the gap matters","explain":"Paragraph 13.13 says a good problem statement uses data and does not bake in a cause or a fix. That work belongs in later steps."},
{"ch":13,"sec":"13A","para":"13.2","title":"Strategic Thinking","page":246,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","question":"Strategic thinking in AFH 1 Chapter 13 asks leaders to look at what horizon?","correct":"How today's choices affect the organization's future position","w1":"Only the next hour of the shift","w2":"Only individual fitness scores","w3":"Only one person's decoration","explain":"Section 13A frames strategic thinking as seeing beyond the immediate task to the organization's future. A single shift report is not the whole view."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14A","para":"14.2","title":"Stages of Team Building","page":260,"diff":1,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1 lists the usual stages of team building in which order?","correct":"Forming, storming, norming, and performing","w1":"Performing, forming, storming, and norming","w2":"Unfreezing, changing, and refreezing","w3":"Planning, programming, budgeting, and execution","explain":"Paragraph 14.2 gives forming, storming, norming, and performing. Lewin's stages and PPBE are different models in other chapters."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14A","para":"14.2","title":"Storming","page":260,"diff":2,"question":"During storming, AFH 1 says team members often do what?","correct":"Resist the team and argue about the task","w1":"Already operate as a smooth, flexible unit","w2":"Skip conflict because conflict is always harmful","w3":"Disband as soon as one person disagrees","explain":"Paragraph 14.2 calls storming the difficult stage of conflict and resistance. Performing is the later stage where the team is actually effective."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14A","para":"14.2","title":"Performing","page":260,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 says a team in the performing stage has reached what condition?","correct":"Members know their roles and can solve problems as a cohesive unit","w1":"Members are still politely unsure of the rules","w2":"Members are in open rebellion and little work happens","w3":"The team has not met yet","explain":"Paragraph 14.2 says performing is when relationships and roles are settled and the team can diagnose problems. Forming is the uncertain start."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14A","para":"14.3","title":"Conflict Management","page":260,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 says conflict becomes destructive when it does what?","correct":"Blocks cooperation and pulls attention off the task","w1":"Surfaces a real disagreement that gets solved","w2":"Improves understanding between groups","w3":"Is named early and worked in the open","explain":"Paragraph 14.3 says conflict can be constructive, and it turns destructive when it builds barriers, hurts morale, and diverts attention from the task."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14D","para":"14.24","title":"Full Range Leadership","page":271,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 says full-range leadership depends on which three elements?","correct":"The leader, the follower, and the situation","w1":"The budget, the aircraft, and the weather","w2":"Rank, ribbons, and time in grade only","w3":"A script that never changes","explain":"Paragraph 14.24 says FRLD is a system of leader, follower, and situation. A fixed script ignores the follower and the moment."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14D","para":"14.25","title":"Laissez-Faire","page":271,"diff":2,"question":"How does AFH 1 describe laissez-faire leadership?","correct":"The leader abandons responsibility and stays indifferent","w1":"The leader monitors closely and corrects early","w2":"The leader sets goals and rewards the contract","w3":"The leader inspires a vision people want to follow","explain":"Paragraph 14.25 calls laissez-faire an abandonment of responsibility. Close monitoring is management by exception-active, and rewards for a set goal are contingent reward."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14D","para":"14.25","title":"Management by Exception","page":271,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1's management-by-exception-passive leader does what?","correct":"Waits until something is broken or a rule is missed, then steps in","w1":"Coaches each person toward a shared vision","w2":"Watches the process constantly to prevent the miss","w3":"Sets a goal and pays the reward when it is met","explain":"Paragraph 14.25 describes passive management by exception as if it is not broken, do not fix it. Active exception watches continuously. Contingent reward is the contract."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14D","para":"14.25","title":"Transformational Leadership","page":271,"diff":3,"question":"Which four components does AFH 1 assign to transformational leadership?","correct":"Individualized consideration, intellectual stimulation, inspirational motivation, and idealized influence","w1":"Laissez-faire, passive exception, active exception, and contingent reward","w2":"Forming, storming, norming, and performing","w3":"Planning, programming, budgeting, and execution","explain":"Paragraph 14.25 names the four I's of transformational leadership. The first distractor is the broader full-range ladder, not the transformational subset."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14D","para":"14.25","title":"Idealized Influence","page":271,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says idealized influence looks like what?","correct":"Leaders act as moral role models and put the group ahead of personal interest","w1":"Leaders disappear until a metric fails","w2":"Leaders pay a bonus only when a quota is hit","w3":"Leaders avoid decisions so followers feel free","explain":"Paragraph 14.25 ties idealized influence to example, character, and the group's interest. Absence and pure transactions are different behaviors on the model."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14B","para":"14.9","title":"Mentoring","page":264,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 treats mentoring as what kind of leader duty?","correct":"A deliberate relationship that develops the other person's career and judgment","w1":"A one-time speech at commander's call","w2":"A replacement for the evaluation system","w3":"An optional favor for favorites only","explain":"Section 14B describes the leader as mentor and the Air Force mentoring program as deliberate development, not a substitute for feedback or a private club."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14B","para":"14.12","title":"Counseling","page":264,"diff":2,"question":"Counseling in AFH 1 is appropriate when a leader needs to do what?","correct":"Address performance, behavior, or a personal situation that affects the mission","w1":"Announce a public ranking of everyone's faults","w2":"Skip documentation because the talk was informal","w3":"Use the session only to issue a decoration","explain":"Section 14B covers when and how to counsel. It is a leadership conversation, not a public shaming and not a decoration ceremony."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14C","para":"14.16","title":"Coaching","page":266,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 distinguishes coaching from a one-way order by saying a coach does what?","correct":"Helps the person build skill through guided practice","w1":"Does the task forever so the person never tries","w2":"Withholds the standard","w3":"Scores the person against a secret rule","explain":"The leadership section describes coaching as developing skill, which requires the standard, practice, and feedback. Doing the job for someone is not coaching."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14A","para":"14.1","title":"Team Building","page":260,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 says all-starring on a team is a problem because it does what?","correct":"Turns cooperation into a contest for personal attention","w1":"Clarifies every member's role","w2":"Reduces power struggles","w3":"Is the definition of performing","explain":"Paragraph 14.1 warns that all-starring is a power struggle. The corrective move is to restate roles so people stop competing for the spotlight."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14D","para":"14.25","title":"Contingent Reward","page":271,"diff":3,"question":"Contingent reward, in AFH 1's full-range model, is which kind of deal?","correct":"The leader sets the goal and reinforces the follower when the standard is met","w1":"The leader ignores results","w2":"The leader inspires through vision alone with no agreement","w3":"The leader punishes at random","explain":"Paragraph 14.25 calls contingent reward a constructive transaction: clear goals, support, and a reward when expectations are met."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14D","para":"14.23","title":"Power","page":271,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says knowledge power comes from what?","correct":"Expertise, often shown by special skill, training, or credentials","w1":"Friendship and trading favors","w2":"The rank on the collar alone","w3":"Being feared","explain":"Paragraph 14.23 separates knowledge power from relationship power and from authority that comes with position. Expertise can move with the person."},
{"ch":14,"sec":"14D","para":"14.26","title":"Leading by Example","page":271,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 says a leader cannot demand the best from others unless the leader does what?","correct":"Shows they are willing to meet the same standard","w1":"Keeps a private exception for themselves","w2":"Speaks louder than they work","w3":"Delegates every hard task and leaves","explain":"Paragraph 14.26 says leading by example means the leader meets the standard they expect. A private exemption destroys the example."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15A","para":"15.1","title":"Accountability","page":279,"diff":1,"demo":True,"question":"How does AFH 1 define accountability?","correct":"Reliability and honesty, plus responsibility for one's own behavior and the team's","w1":"Blaming the last shift","w2":"Keeping quiet so the metric survives","w3":"Loyalty to a friend over the standard","explain":"Paragraph 15.1 defines accountability as reliability, honesty, and responsibility for self and team. Hiding a miss is the opposite of that definition."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15A","para":"15.1","title":"Accountability","page":279,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 says surveys of Airmen have rated which foundational competency most important to a career?","correct":"Accountability","w1":"Digital literacy","w2":"Precision of uniform fold lines","w3":"Speed of email replies","explain":"Paragraph 15.1 says accountability has consistently been rated the most important foundational competency for success in an Air Force career."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15A","para":"15.1","title":"Accountability","page":279,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says taking personal responsibility requires which three ideas?","correct":"Significance, self-efficacy, and autonomy","w1":"Rank, time in grade, and a decoration","w2":"Silence, speed, and luck","w3":"A vote, a meeting, and a slide","explain":"Paragraph 15.1 walks through significance of the situation, belief in one's ability to act, and the freedom to choose. Rank is not the substitute."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15A","para":"15.2","title":"Perseverance","page":279,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 contrasts a fixed mindset with a growth mindset. A growth mindset believes what?","correct":"Ability can change through effort, and early failure does not define the person","w1":"Talent is permanent, so effort is embarrassing","w2":"Only the first score counts","w3":"Feedback is a threat to be avoided","explain":"Paragraph 15.2 says a growth mindset treats ability as changeable and uses effort and feedback. A fixed mindset treats early failure as proof of a permanent limit."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15A","para":"15.2","title":"Perseverance","page":279,"diff":3,"question":"People with a fixed mindset, as AFH 1 describes them, tend to do what when the work gets hard?","correct":"Quit early and treat effort as evidence they lack ability","w1":"Ask for feedback and try a new approach","w2":"Treat the miss as data","w3":"Study the task longer","explain":"Paragraph 15.2 says fixed-mindset people give up and read effort as proof of inability. Persistence and use of feedback belong to the growth description."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15A","para":"15.3","title":"Self-Control","page":279,"diff":2,"question":"Self-control in AFH 1 starts with what skill?","correct":"Noticing your own emotion and choosing a response that does not harm the mission","w1":"Never feeling frustration","w2":"Raising your voice so the room understands","w3":"Leaving the job unfinished until you feel better","explain":"The developing-self section ties self-control to managing emotion under stress, not to the absence of emotion or to dumping it on the team."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15A","para":"15.4","title":"Resilience","page":279,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 describes resilience as the ability to do what?","correct":"Adapt and recover when stress or trauma hits, then continue the mission","w1":"Pretend the stress is not real","w2":"Refuse help because strength means isolation","w3":"Avoid every hard assignment","explain":"Resilience in the foundational-competency sense is recovery and adaptation, not denial and not isolation from help."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15B","para":"15.5","title":"Communication","page":284,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 treats communication as a foundational competency because Airmen must do what?","correct":"Present, listen, and tailor the message to the audience","w1":"Use the longest sentence available","w2":"Hide bad news in jargon","w3":"Speak only to people of equal rank","explain":"Section 15B frames communication as clear messaging and active listening, matched to the audience. Jargon that hides the point fails that test."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15B","para":"15.11","title":"Phonetic Alphabet","page":284,"diff":2,"question":"Why does AFH 1 include the military phonetic alphabet in the communication chapter?","correct":"So spoken letters are not mistaken in noise or on the radio","w1":"So evaluations can be written in code","w2":"To replace plain language in every email","w3":"As a secret promotion signal","explain":"Paragraph 15.11 presents the phonetic alphabet as a clarity tool. It is not a code for records or a rank signal."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15C","para":"15.15","title":"Seven Steps for Communication","page":288,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1's communication steps exist so a speaker does what before they talk?","correct":"Purpose, audience, and organization are decided before the first sentence","w1":"The speaker starts and hopes the point appears","w2":"The audience is ignored until the end","w3":"Slides are read word for word with no aim","explain":"Section 15C pushes preparation: know why you are communicating and to whom. Improvising the purpose mid-sentence is how briefs wander."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15D","para":"15.19","title":"Official Memorandum","page":293,"diff":2,"question":"An official memorandum in AFH 1 is used for what kind of writing?","correct":"Directed official communication inside the service","w1":"A private social media argument","w2":"A travel-card receipt","w3":"A fitness worksheet","explain":"Section 15D distinguishes official memorandums and other Air Force papers from casual messages. The format signals that the content is an official act."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15E","para":"15.31","title":"Effective Listening","page":295,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 includes listening in spoken communication because a leader who only talks will do what?","correct":"Miss information the team already has","w1":"Automatically become more accountable","w2":"Satisfy the phonetic alphabet requirement","w3":"Complete a staff package","explain":"Paragraph 15.31 treats listening as part of communication, not a courtesy. Decisions made without it throw away facts the team already knows."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15F","para":"15.33","title":"Government Systems","page":300,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1 says limited personal use of government communications must be what?","correct":"Reasonable in duration and frequency, approved, and not harmful to the duty or the service","w1":"Unlimited if it happens after 1500","w2":"Allowed for campaigning and private business","w3":"Fine for storing classified data on a home device","explain":"The electronic-messaging section allows only reasonable, approved personal use that does not hurt performance, overload systems, or discredit the service."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15F","para":"15.34","title":"Internet Use","page":300,"diff":3,"question":"Which use does AFH 1 specifically prohibit on government communications?","correct":"Storing or sending pornography, gambling, extremist material, or classified data on a system not approved for it","w1":"Sending an official tasking to the office mailbox","w2":"Reading a signed order","w3":"Using the phonetic alphabet on a radio net","explain":"The prohibited-use list in the electronic communications section includes pornography, gambling, extremist content, and classified processing on unapproved systems."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15B","para":"15.9","title":"Social Media","page":284,"diff":3,"question":"AFH 1's social-media guidance exists so Airmen remember what?","correct":"Online speech can still harm the mission, good order, and the public's trust","w1":"Personal accounts are invisible to the service","w2":"Operational details are fine if the post is funny","w3":"The handbook encourages sharing troop movements","explain":"Section 15B discusses social media as communication with public consequences. Humor does not declassify a detail or cancel standards."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15E","para":"15.29","title":"Speaking Anxiety","page":295,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 treats fear of speaking as what kind of problem?","correct":"A common, trainable fear, not proof the person cannot lead","w1":"A permanent disqualification from NCO duty","w2":"Evidence of low integrity","w3":"A reason to skip all feedback sessions","explain":"The speaking section calls public speaking a common self-induced fear and offers delivery techniques. It is not a character finding."},
{"ch":15,"sec":"15D","para":"15.22","title":"Staff Work","page":293,"diff":3,"question":"A staff package in AFH 1 is meant to do what?","correct":"Give a decision-maker a complete, organized recommendation","w1":"Replace the spoken brief with raw notes","w2":"Hide the recommendation until the last slide","w3":"List opinions with no course of action","explain":"Section 15D describes staff papers and packages as tools that frame a decision. A pile of unsorted opinions is not a package."},
]
